    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d265-a1">

                <pb facs="0276" n="954" sameAs="frus:frus1863p2/pg954"/>


                <head>Mr. Burlingame to Mr. Cunningham</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Peking</hi>,</placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1863-03-25"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >March</hi> 25, 1863.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> I have the honor to inform you that
                    immediately upon the receipt of your humane suggestion, to improve the sanitary
                    condition of Shanghai by the construction of a new gate, and the drainage of
                    stagnant water from before the city, I applied to the authorities at Peking, and
                    requested that they would inquire of the local authorities of Shanghai as to the
                    necessity for these improvements, and, if the Chinese desired, to permit them to
                    be made. I was at once informed that instructions would be sent to the local
                    authorities to have the improvements made, if they thought best. I asked them if
                    I should make known to you these instructions. They said yes. I accordingly
                    inform you of the same, and express the gratification I feel in being able to do
                    something for the health of the people of Shanghai.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>ANSON BURLINGAME.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Edward Cunningham</hi>, Esq.</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
